	"identifying_description": "Woman's red synthetic tulle and synthetic and metallic lace dress with cotton embroidery (satin stitch), sequins, and beads; cap sleeves with v-neckline and long full skirt; accented throughout with floral-motif embellishments, including synthetic net and lace rosettes with sequins and beaded fringe; silk lining with zipper and hook and eye closure at back; tag at center back (gray text on white label): \"RODARTE\"",
	"web_text": "Nature remains an enduring revelation to Laura and Kate Mulleavy, the founders of Rodarte. Born in California, the sisters often draw inspiration from the landscape of the state, from the hot and dry climes of Death Valley to the lush floral gardens of Northern California, where they grew up. The Mulleavys’ Spring/ Summer 2018 collection exemplified their ongoing revelry with the natural world as well as their signature romantic aesthetic. The designs were influenced by the female archetypes of director Robert Altman’s 1977 film, 3 Women, described by the sisters as the dreamer, the artist, and the intellectual. Semi-sheer silk tulle, lace, and net gowns were delicately embellished with sequins, paillettes, and beads to form a variety of burgeoning flowers. This gown was worn by longtime Rodarte client Christine Suppes to the ninety-sixth annual San Francisco Opera Ball at the War Memorial Opera House in 2018. It features clusters of poppies, a plant species native to California, and also a symbol of remembrance, while many others in the collection were festooned with baby’s breath, a symbol of purity and youth. The collection’s unveiling marked Rodarte’s first-ever runway show in Paris, held at the exclusive invitation of the Chambre Syndicale de la Haute Couture, the governing body of the French fashion industry, during couture fashion week.",
